{"slug":"no-two-adjacent-ones","title":"Binary Strings Without Two Ones in a Row","topic":"backtracking","difficulty":"easy","order":416,"tags":["backtracking","strings","pruning"],"timeLimitMs":2000,"concept":{"title":"Check the rule before the choice, not after the string is done","body":["To generate all binary strings of length `n`, decide the characters from left to right: every position has two choices, `0` and `1`. Here a rule is added: two neighbouring characters must not both be `1`. The rule only concerns the **last** character placed and the new one, so it can be checked immediately.",{"code":"def build(n, current, out):\n    if len(current) == n:\n        out.append(\"\".join(current))\n        return\n    current.append(\"0\")                  # a 0 is always allowed\n    build(n, current, out)\n    current.pop()\n    if not current or current[-1] != \"1\":   # a 1 only after a 0 or at the start\n        current.append(\"1\")\n        build(n, current, out)\n        current.pop()"},"Strings that break the rule are never started, so no time is lost on them. Trying `0` before `1` produces the strings in increasing order. The number of valid strings follows the Fibonacci numbers (for `n = 14` there are 987), far fewer than the `2^14 = 16384` strings of all kinds."]},"statement":["Print all binary strings of length `n` (made of `0` and `1`) that do not contain two `1` characters next to each other. Print them in increasing order (as binary strings), one per line."],"inputFormat":["The only line has `n`."],"outputFormat":["The strings in increasing order, one per line."],"constraints":["1 <= n <= 14"],"examples":[{"input":"3\n","output":"000\n001\n010\n100\n101\n","note":"The valid strings are 000, 001, 010, 100 and 101."}],"hints":["Build the string from left to right; a 0 can always be added.","A 1 can be added only if the string is empty or ends with a 0.","Try 0 first and then 1 to get the strings in increasing order."],"starter":{"python":"n = int(input())\n\n# Your code here\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    // Your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        // Your code here\n    }\n}\n"},"solution":{"explanation":["The string grows by one character per call. A `0` is always allowed and a `1` only when the string is empty or ends with `0`. Trying `0` first gives increasing order."],"complexity":"Time O(F(n) * n), where F(n) is the number of valid strings; space O(n) besides the output.","code":{"python":"def build(n, current, out):\n    if len(current) == n:\n        out.append(\"\".join(current))\n        return\n    current.append(\"0\")\n    build(n, current, out)\n    current.pop()\n    if not current or current[-1] != \"1\":\n        current.append(\"1\")\n        build(n, current, out)\n        current.pop()\n\nn = int(input())\n\nout = []\nbuild(n, [], out)\nprint(\"\\n\".join(out))\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid build(int n, string& current, vector<string>& out) {\n    if ((int)current.size() == n) {\n        out.push_back(current);\n        return;\n    }\n    current.push_back('0');\n    build(n, current, out);\n    current.pop_back();\n    if (current.empty() || current.back() != '1') {\n        current.push_back('1');\n        build(n, current, out);\n        current.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    vector<string> out;\n    string current;\n    build((int)n, current, out);\n    for (const string& s : out) cout << s << \"\\n\";\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void build(int n, StringBuilder current, List<String> out) {\n        if (current.length() == n) {\n            out.add(current.toString());\n            return;\n        }\n        current.append('0');\n        build(n, current, out);\n        current.deleteCharAt(current.length() - 1);\n        if (current.length() == 0 || current.charAt(current.length() - 1) != '1') {\n            current.append('1');\n            build(n, current, out);\n            current.deleteCharAt(current.length() - 1);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        build((int) n, new StringBuilder(), out);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (String s : out) sb.append(s).append(\"\\n\");\n        System.out.print(sb);\n    }\n}\n"}},"tests":[{"input":"1\n","output":"0\n1\n"},{"input":"2\n","output":"00\n01\n10\n"},{"input":"3\n","output":"000\n001\n010\n100\n101\n"},{"input":"4\n","output":"0000\n0001\n0010\n0100\n0101\n1000\n1001\n1010\n"},{"input":"5\n","output":"00000\n00001\n00010\n00100\n00101\n01000\n01001\n01010\n10000\n10001\n10010\n10100\n10101\n"},{"input":"6\n","output":"000000\n000001\n000010\n000100\n000101\n001000\n001001\n001010\n010000\n010001\n010010\n010100\n010101\n100000\n100001\n100010\n100100\n100101\n101000\n101001\n101010\n"},{"input":"8\n","output":"00000000\n00000001\n00000010\n00000100\n00000101\n00001000\n00001001\n00001010\n00010000\n00010001\n00010010\n00010100\n00010101\n00100000\n00100001\n00100010\n00100100\n00100101\n00101000\n00101001\n00101010\n01000000\n01000001\n01000010\n01000100\n01000101\n01001000\n01001001\n01001010\n01010000\n01010001\n01010010\n01010100\n01010101\n10000000\n10000001\n10000010\n10000100\n10000101\n10001000\n10001001\n10001010\n10010000\n10010001\n10010010\n10010100\n10010101\n10100000\n10100001\n10100010\n10100100\n10100101\n10101000\n10101001\n10101010\n"},{"input":"10\n","output":"0000000000\n0000000001\n0000000010\n0000000100\n0000000101\n0000